+==============================
+Parcel FreeBSD policy studies
+==============================
+
+Parcel Play OS uses selected FreeBSD ``sys/kern`` files as sources of test
+hypotheses. FreeBSD internal APIs are not a Linux compatibility layer and
+source files must not be copied wholesale.
+
+Translation map
+===============
+
+The preferred Linux 7.1 mechanisms are:
+
+* boot tracing: bootconfig, ftrace and initcall tracepoints;
+* failure tests: Linux fault injection in a laboratory kernel;
+* ULE policy research: sched_ext BPF programs with safe fallback;
+* sendfile and TLS: Linux sendfile, splice and kTLS;
+* Jail and VNET isolation: namespaces and cgroup v2;
+* Capsicum-like restriction: seccomp, Landlock, capabilities and LSMs;
+* name cache research: dcache, RCU-walk and pathname lookup profiling;
+* Windows synchronization: the in-tree NTSYNC driver.
+
+NTSYNC must not be reimplemented by Parcel. ULE runqueues and FreeBSD locks
+must not be copied into the Linux scheduler.
+
+Evidence before code
+====================
+
+A patch requires a reproducible Linux problem. Architectural differences are
+not evidence by themselves. Prefer tracing, selftests, configuration and
+userspace policy before source changes.
+
+Production and laboratory kernels
+=================================
+
+Fault injection, KASAN, KCSAN and KCOV belong to separate laboratory builds.
+They must not be enabled automatically in production or gaming kernels.
+Destructive tests require a disposable VM and recoverable disk image.
+
+Scheduler studies
+=================
+
+Start with EEVDF as the baseline. A policy inspired by ULE should use
+sched_ext, pass upstream sched_ext selftests and return tasks safely to the
+built-in scheduler on exit or error. Core scheduler patches are a last resort.
+
+Small algorithms
+================
+
+PID controllers, filters and statistical helpers remain in userspace until a
+real in-kernel consumer exists. Kernel code must avoid floating point, define
+units and overflow behaviour, use existing Linux helpers and include KUnit
+tests.
+
+Licensing
+=========
+
+Every derived fragment needs per-file licence review. Copyright notices and
+attribution must be retained when required. Independent implementation from
+documented behaviour and tests is preferred.
+
+Acceptance gates
+================
+
+Functional patches require a verified upstream tag or commit, clean build,
+relevant selftests, VM boot, security review, performance statistics and a
+documented rollback. A known-good vanilla kernel remains a boot fallback.
+Experiments without measurable value stay outside the production series.
